{"_about":"Example hook definition for the auto-recovery guard (tutorial Part 4). Save it with your runtime's hook definitions (on the author's runtime: ~/hooks/definitions/ssh-tunnel-ensure.json) and adjust the paths. The definition itself lives inside $HOME on purpose: a VM reset wipes /etc (systemd units included) but never $HOME, so the scheduler survives exactly the disaster it exists to fix.","enabled":true,"id":"ssh-tunnel-ensure","poll_interval_secs":30,"prompt":"PLACEHOLDER — replace this with the wake-up instructions for YOUR agent. In the author's setup this prompt tells the agent to stay silent on healthy polls and to act on three events only, at most once per event: (1) event \"auto-repaired\" — the guard script already fixed the VM side by itself after a reset; the agent just verifies quickly (Tailscale Connected, the supervisor pid alive, sshd listening on the local port) and sends the owner ONE short message that the SSH door is back on its own and they can reconnect with: ssh -i muse-key -p 2223 root@127.0.0.1. (2) event \"tailscale-down\" — Tailscale has been disconnected for 5+ minutes and no script can repair that; the agent gets a fresh approval link (tailscale down, then tailscale up) and sends that link to the owner — or sends nothing if it turns out to be Connected again. (3) event \"repair-failed\" — the automatic repair failed (details in the payload); the agent diagnoses once from the project logs (sshd.log, reverse.log), fixes manually if it can, and reports the result briefly. Never message the owner about routine healthy checks, and never send duplicates.","script_path":"~/hooks/scripts/ensure-hook.sh","script_timeout_secs":600,"version":1}